WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.534 --> 00:00:03.737
Many popular films
today feature animals

00:00:03.737 --> 00:00:06.439
or otherworldly creatures
as a main character.

00:00:06.706 --> 00:00:07.874
Behind these lifelike
performances

00:00:07.874 --> 00:00:09.409
is a real human actor

00:00:09.409 --> 00:00:09.476
bringing an authenticity
to these creatures

00:00:09.476 --> 00:00:09.542
bringing an authenticity
to these creatures

00:00:09.542 --> 00:00:09.609
bringing an authenticity
to these creatures

00:00:09.609 --> 00:00:11.311
bringing an authenticity
to these creatures

00:00:11.311 --> 00:00:13.079
that CGI can't replicate.

00:00:13.079 --> 00:00:15.849
These actors bring a specific
physicality to a character

00:00:15.849 --> 00:00:18.218
and on set, give
a real performance

00:00:18.218 --> 00:00:21.087
that other actors can
react to in real time.

00:00:21.087 --> 00:00:22.989
That performance then
becomes the framework

00:00:22.989 --> 00:00:25.959
that postproduction builds
a fuller character around.

00:00:25.959 --> 00:00:28.528
These performances are captured
using a green-screen suit,

00:00:28.528 --> 00:00:30.897
a motion-capture suit,
or a blend of the two,

00:00:30.897 --> 00:00:32.866
with practical makeup
and costumes.

00:00:32.866 --> 00:00:35.068
Here are seven films that
use creature performers

00:00:35.068 --> 00:00:37.537
in creative ways to
elevate the story.

00:00:40.573 --> 00:00:43.176
Creature performer Terry
Notary wore a mo-cap suit

00:00:43.176 --> 00:00:45.278
to capture his physicality
as Buck the dog

00:00:45.278 --> 00:00:46.880
in "The Call of the Wild."

00:00:46.880 --> 00:00:49.449
Having Notary on set giving
a committed performance

00:00:49.449 --> 00:00:51.351
was said to help
elevate the performances

00:00:51.351 --> 00:00:53.520
of the actors he
shared a screen with.

00:00:53.520 --> 00:00:56.656
As a creature actor, Notary
was more that just a stand-in.

00:00:56.656 --> 00:00:59.192
He often conferred with the
director and other actors,

00:00:59.192 --> 00:01:01.461
working through shots
and scenes with them.

00:01:02.929 --> 00:01:04.064
To train for the role,

00:01:04.064 --> 00:01:06.132
Notary filmed the
movements of his own dogs

00:01:06.132 --> 00:01:07.634
and watched the
footage in slow motion

00:01:07.634 --> 00:01:10.070
to understand how they walk.

00:01:10.070 --> 00:01:12.305
Actor Harrison Ford
praised Notary's work,

00:01:12.305 --> 00:01:13.940
saying it was important
to have someone

00:01:13.940 --> 00:01:16.443
to participate emotionally
with on screen.

00:01:21.081 --> 00:01:23.049
For Disney's live-action
"Dumbo" remake,

00:01:23.049 --> 00:01:24.317
the beloved baby elephant

00:01:24.317 --> 00:01:26.319
was made completely in CGI.

00:01:26.319 --> 00:01:28.855
But to get believable character
movements and emotion,

00:01:28.855 --> 00:01:31.791
creature performer Edd Osmond
wore a specialized green suit

00:01:31.791 --> 00:01:33.259
to capture
authentic movements

00:01:33.259 --> 00:01:35.762
and interactions
with fellow actors.

00:01:35.762 --> 00:01:35.829
Osmond spent hours observing
how elephants walk

00:01:35.895 --> 00:01:38.865
And for certain shots,
a stunt performer stepped in.

00:01:39.332 --> 00:01:40.166
Osmond spent hours observing
how elephants walk

00:01:40.166 --> 00:01:42.068
Osmond spent hours observing
how elephants walk

00:01:42.068 --> 00:01:42.202
Osmond spent hours observing
how elephants walk

00:01:42.202 --> 00:01:42.268
Osmond spent hours observing
how elephants walk

00:01:42.268 --> 00:01:42.402
Osmond spent hours observing
how elephants walk

00:01:42.402 --> 00:01:43.970
and trained himself
to look believable

00:01:43.970 --> 00:01:46.639
while hunched over
on all fours in stilts.

00:01:48.508 --> 00:01:48.875
Osmond also had to consider that
this was a baby elephant,

00:01:48.875 --> 00:01:51.277
Osmond also had to consider that
this was a baby elephant,

00:01:51.845 --> 00:01:53.980
so movements had to
be a bit less confident

00:01:53.980 --> 00:01:54.948
and more sporadic.

00:01:55.115 --> 00:01:55.448
To play the character, Osmond
wore multiple different suits.

00:01:55.448 --> 00:01:56.116
To play the character, Osmond
wore multiple different suits.

00:01:56.116 --> 00:01:58.685
To play the character, Osmond
wore multiple different suits.

00:01:58.685 --> 00:01:58.718
First, a performance suit
that looks like the character

00:01:58.885 --> 00:02:01.121
First, a performance suit
that looks like the character

00:02:01.121 --> 00:02:02.789
for actors to interact with.

00:02:02.789 --> 00:02:05.191
Then there was the bare-bones
"ant-man" costume

00:02:05.191 --> 00:02:08.695
used when Dumbo was
running or walking around.

00:02:08.795 --> 00:02:08.995
Since Osmond's head
was pointing down,

00:02:08.995 --> 00:02:09.629
Since Osmond's head
was pointing down,

00:02:09.629 --> 00:02:10.697
Since Osmond's head
was pointing down,

00:02:10.964 --> 00:02:12.699
a camera was mounted
on the top of his head

00:02:12.699 --> 00:02:14.567
so he could see
where he was going.

00:02:20.206 --> 00:02:22.442
In some instances,
a more well-known actor

00:02:22.442 --> 00:02:24.310
will do their own
creature performances.

00:02:24.310 --> 00:02:24.344
"Fleabag" star Phoebe
Waller-Bridge

00:02:24.344 --> 00:02:26.613
"Fleabag" star Phoebe
Waller-Bridge

00:02:26.613 --> 00:02:27.981
did her on set performance

00:02:27.981 --> 00:02:31.084
as L3-37, wearing
a modified robot suit

00:02:31.084 --> 00:02:33.820
that was part practical
and part green screen suit.

00:02:33.820 --> 00:02:35.455
As a uniquely funny performer,

00:02:35.455 --> 00:02:37.357
it needed to be
Waller-Bridge on set

00:02:37.357 --> 00:02:39.726
alongside Donald Glover,
who played Lando,

00:02:39.726 --> 00:02:42.629
to capture the right chemistry
between the two characters.

00:02:42.629 --> 00:02:45.899
Her L3-37 performance was
modified in postproduction

00:02:45.899 --> 00:02:48.535
to look more like a robot,
but kept the same physicality

00:02:48.535 --> 00:02:49.769
Waller-Bridge
performed on set.

00:02:54.541 --> 00:02:55.942
Dozens of
creature performers,

00:02:55.942 --> 00:02:58.111
including Andy Serkis
and Terry Notary,

00:02:58.111 --> 00:03:00.780
wore modified mo-cap suits
when performing as apes

00:03:00.780 --> 00:03:02.649
in the "Planet of
the Apes" trilogy.

00:03:03.683 --> 00:03:06.286
Finding a way to deliver
believable ape performances

00:03:06.286 --> 00:03:08.254
led production
to Weta studios.

00:03:08.254 --> 00:03:09.789
Using new
technology at the time,

00:03:09.789 --> 00:03:10.056
2011's "Rise of the
Planet of the Apes"

00:03:10.056 --> 00:03:10.790
2011's "Rise of the
Planet of the Apes"

00:03:10.790 --> 00:03:12.358
2011's "Rise of the
Planet of the Apes"

00:03:12.358 --> 00:03:12.392
became the first production
to do motion capture

00:03:12.392 --> 00:03:14.894
became the first production
to do motion capture

00:03:14.894 --> 00:03:16.529
in a live-action setting.

00:03:16.529 --> 00:03:19.299
Then in 2014's "Dawn of the
Planet of the Apes",

00:03:19.299 --> 00:03:21.701
they set up over
50 motion-capture cameras

00:03:21.701 --> 00:03:23.770
throughout the sets,
all sending the footage

00:03:23.770 --> 00:03:25.405
over WiFi to remote servers

00:03:25.405 --> 00:03:27.407
so there was no wires to hide.

00:03:27.407 --> 00:03:29.776
Then in "War of the
Planet of the Apes" they pushed

00:03:29.776 --> 00:03:31.177
even further by shooting

00:03:31.177 --> 00:03:32.378
motion capture in the snow.

00:03:32.378 --> 00:03:34.180
but creature performers
walking on crutches

00:03:34.180 --> 00:03:37.083
instead of hands and having
different gaits than real apes

00:03:37.283 --> 00:03:39.552
meant entire paths
and foot print patterns

00:03:39.552 --> 00:03:41.287
had to be
digitally recreated.

00:03:44.457 --> 00:03:46.659
Speaking of actor chemistry,
Dan Stevens,

00:03:46.659 --> 00:03:48.895
who played the Beast in the
Disney live-action remake

00:03:48.895 --> 00:03:49.896
of "Beauty and the Beast,"

00:03:49.896 --> 00:03:52.899
wore a Beast mo-cap suit to
act alongside Emma Watson.

00:03:52.899 --> 00:03:53.233
wore a Beast mo-cap suit to
act alongside Emma Watson.

00:03:53.233 --> 00:03:53.666
wore a Beast mo-cap suit to
act alongside Emma Watson.

00:03:53.666 --> 00:03:55.768
It included stilts to
be the proper height

00:03:55.768 --> 00:03:57.570
and weighed over 40 pounds.

00:03:57.570 --> 00:04:00.206
Stevens noted that the suit
was particularly difficult

00:04:00.206 --> 00:04:01.975
to act in during
the waltzing scene

00:04:01.975 --> 00:04:04.110
and said he feels lucky
he didn't lose his balance

00:04:04.110 --> 00:04:06.246
and fall onto Watson.

00:04:08.815 --> 00:04:10.483
Not all creature
performers are used as

00:04:10.483 --> 00:04:12.285
only a framework for CGI.

00:04:12.285 --> 00:04:15.188
In the instance of "Scary
Stories to Tell in the Dark,"

00:04:15.188 --> 00:04:18.024
creature performers
Javier Botet, Troy James,

00:04:18.024 --> 00:04:20.493
and Mark Steger all
wore practical costumes

00:04:20.493 --> 00:04:23.096
that incorporated their specific
body types and movements

00:04:23.096 --> 00:04:25.565
to bring the iconic
monsters to life.

00:04:25.565 --> 00:04:27.500
The unique physicality
of character actors

00:04:27.500 --> 00:04:28.768
was used for the movement

00:04:28.768 --> 00:04:31.871
of the on-screen
monsters they played.

00:04:31.871 --> 00:04:34.507
The costumes were created
with painstaking detail

00:04:34.507 --> 00:04:38.011
by Spectral Motion
specifically to be on camera.

00:04:38.011 --> 00:04:40.013
Botet's costume
even incorporated

00:04:40.013 --> 00:04:41.514
a slight green-suit element

00:04:41.514 --> 00:04:44.317
for his character's big
toe to be removed in post.

00:04:48.054 --> 00:04:49.789
Another instance where
the character actor's

00:04:49.789 --> 00:04:51.824
performance was
almost all practical

00:04:51.824 --> 00:04:54.127
is from Doug Jones in
"The Shape of Water."

00:04:54.127 --> 00:04:56.596
Jones, who's worked with
director Guillermo del Toro

00:04:56.596 --> 00:05:00.099
on several films, including
as Faun in "Pan's Labyrinth,"

00:05:00.099 --> 00:05:02.435
played the Amphibian Man
in a practical costume

00:05:02.435 --> 00:05:04.704
with elements of digital
retouching added later,

00:05:04.704 --> 00:05:06.339
like blinking
and micro gestures.

00:05:06.339 --> 00:05:07.674
Even the gills were practical,

00:05:07.674 --> 00:05:10.009
mechanically
operated by puppeteers.

00:05:10.009 --> 00:05:12.278
The glowing bioluminescent
aspects of the suit

00:05:12.278 --> 00:05:15.181
were also practical,
using UV paint.

00:05:15.181 --> 00:05:16.549
Del Toro decided to have

00:05:16.549 --> 00:05:18.251
the amphibian
character be practical

00:05:18.251 --> 00:05:19.819
because it was the
main character

00:05:19.819 --> 00:05:21.721
and the story's
emotional center.

00:05:21.721 --> 00:05:23.489
Therefore, Jones
had to perform

00:05:23.489 --> 00:05:25.391
to give it the
character's soul.

